# Lines matching any regex here are dropped from scripts/scan-secrets.sh output.
#
# Every entry needs a reason. This file is the only thing that lets a 12-digit
# number through, so an unexplained line here is a hole. Prefer narrow entries:
# an allow that covers a shape ("any UUID") also covers a real value that
# happens to sit on the same line.

# --- AWS's own documentation placeholders -----------------------------------
# https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/manage-acct-identifiers.html
\b(123456789012|111122223333|444455556666|777788889999|555555555555)\b

# Repeated-digit ids used as throwaway values in unit tests. No real AWS
# account is a single repeated digit.
\b(0{12}|1{12}|2{12}|3{12}|4{12}|5{12}|6{12}|7{12}|8{12}|9{12})\b

# --- Demo fixture -----------------------------------------------------------
# docs/demo/fixture.json numbers its synthetic accounts 100000000000 upward.
# The range is reserved for the fixture; nothing real may use it.
\b1000000000[0-9][0-9]\b

# A CodeBuild execution UUID in the fixture whose last group happens to be 12
# digits: "...-84ad-284392220146". Listed as the literal value rather than as
# "any UUID" on purpose — a shape-wide allow would also hide a real account id
# printed on the same line. If re-recording the fixture trips this rule again,
# look at the new value before adding it.
284392220146

# --- The scanner's own source -----------------------------------------------
# scan-secrets.sh has to spell out the strings it refuses, so it matches
# itself. Allowed per line rather than per file: exempting the whole file
# would make it the one place a real value could hide.
^scripts/scan-secrets\.sh:[0-9]+:(#|rule )

# --- Email --------------------------------------------------------------
# example.com is reserved by RFC 2606 for documentation. The fixture and the
# golden files use aws+<name>@example.com.
@example\.com
# GoReleaser's commit author for the Homebrew tap.
@users\.noreply\.github\.com
